A commit gate and a wait until scheduled for the Metal backend (ADR-0029, decisions 8 and 15).

Why: iOS refuses GPU work in the background, and Minato promises that no app reaches the GPU there,
whatever it does (ADR-0032, decision 6). Every command buffer ANGLE commits (presents, glFlush,
glFinish, reads, its own flushes) now first calls a function that the embedder sets with
ANGLEMetalSetCommitGates(void (*gate)(void), void (*counted)(void)); Minato's graphics/host holds the
commit there while the app is in the background. The second function comes once ANGLE counted the
command buffer as committed, so the embedder knows every commit between the two: a commit that passed
the gate just before the app went into the background is either counted already or still known to the
embedder when it asks for "scheduled" (ADR-0029 B1). ANGLEMetalWaitUntilScheduled(double seconds)
returns 1 once every command buffer committed so far, on every display's queue, is scheduled, and 0
after the timeout. It takes only the queues' own locks, so a host thread can call it while a guest
thread waits at the gate inside ANGLE.
Upstream: no; it serves one embedder's lifecycle.
Remove: not while Minato keeps apps off the GPU in the background.

